  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 PM CDT Thu May 28 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nBlocking ridge continues across much of the eastern Plains and\nUpper Midwest, and that will change only slightly over the next\nfew days. Most models have a lead shortwave moving into Neb/SD\ntomorrow and then lifting northeast into our area Friday night\nand Saturday. However, this shortwave and the one following it\nlater during the weekend will be limited in impacts by the\nblocking ridge just to our east. A stronger lead shortwave\ncoming out next week looks like it will actually be able to\nstart to break down the upper ridge by Wednesday and Thursday,\nand at that point we should actually get the surface trough\nmoving through instead of staying to our west and washing out.\nHowever, with any shift out of a blocking pattern the details\nremain very uncertain. Timing and intensity of any precipitation\nis unknown at this point, but continued temperatures above\nseasonal averages seems likely given ridging to southwesterly\nflow. \n\n...Fire weather concerns tomorrow...\n\nSouth to southeasterly winds will be a bit more breezy tomorrow\nin eastern ND as surface high pressure pulls further southeast \nand the trough over the northern Rockies deepens. There will be \nincreasing high clouds as a shortwave approaches out of SD/Neb, \nbut with good low level mixing the temps still look very toasty \nfor tomorrow. NBM has a 60 to 80 percent probability for above \n90 highs in the Red River Valley and along our eastern CWA \nborder. The box and whiskers plot has highs for Fargo tomorrow \nranging from 84 to 94 at the 10th and 90th percentiles \nrespectively, so above average no matter what. Probabilities for\nRH values dropping below 25 percent in northwestern MN have \ngone down a bit and are around 20 percent, but if temps end up \non the higher side of the spread the RH values could get a bit \nlower. Winds will not be very strong east of the Red River, so\nwill continue to keep near critical mention. Our western\ncounties will be up around 20 mph but RHs should be in the 30 to\n35 percent range in that area, so again near critical and not\nRed Flag. The Red River Valley is expected to have lowest RH \nvalues in the 25 to 30 percent range, and winds will be just\nunder 20 mph. Will watch that area in case temps or winds get\nhigher than expected. \n\n...Shower and thunderstorm chances...\n\nA few of the models break out some showers with the SD shortwave\nlate Friday into Friday night and approach our southeastern ND\ncounties. Model soundings have a lot of dry air to overcome at\nlow levels so don't think too much will reach the ground. There\nis some weak instability and HREF has some spotty 30 percent\nprobabilities of thunder tomorrow afternoon and evening, so \ncan't rule out some lightning but at this point nothing severe\nis expected. Additional shortwaves trying to break into the\nblocking ridge could bring a few stray showers and thunderstorms\nwest of the Red River Valley at times over the weekend, but the\nbetter chances for more widespread precipitation look to arrive\nearly to mid-week. A few of the machine learning runs have some\nlow probabilities of severe storms for this period, but\npredictability too low to mention in any messaging at this \npoint.",
